"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:10,056\nDo quizzes cause anxiety?\n\n4\n00:00:12,856 --> 00:00:16,726\nMany instructors avoid frequent quizzes for a kind reason.\n\n5\n00:00:17,093 --> 00:00:19,881\nThey worry that more tests mean more stress.\n\n6\n00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:21,566\nWhat does the evidence say?\n\n7\n00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:24,964\nIn 2023, Chunliang Yang,\n\n8\n00:00:25,232 --> 00:00:30,565\nDavid Shanks and colleagues pooled twenty four studies with more than three thousand students,\n\n9\n00:00:30,926 --> 00:00:32,809\nmost in real classrooms,\n\n10\n00:00:33,085 --> 00:00:37,405\nthat measured test anxiety before and after a period of regular quizzing.\n\n11\n00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:42,777\nOn average, practice tests reduced test anxiety,\n\n12\n00:00:43,264 --> 00:00:46,698\nby a medium amount: an effect size of about minus zero point five.\n\n13\n00:00:47,359 --> 00:00:49,944\nThere was little sign of publication bias,\n\n14\n00:00:50,212 --> 00:00:56,978\nand the result held when the authors removed the one study that measured anxiety during the quizzes themselves.\n\n15\n00:00:57,148 --> 00:01:01,524\nThe benefit was larger when quizzes were easier and lower stakes.\n\n16\n00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:05,278\nRetakes and ungraded quizzes are good examples.\n\n17\n00:01:05,448 --> 00:01:07,461\nWhy would tests calm students?\n\n18\n00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:13,002\nOne idea is familiarity: the exam format stops being a surprise.\n\n19\n00:01:13,328 --> 00:01:16,572\nAnother is that students simply know the material better,\n\n20\n00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:18,245\nand know that they know it.\n\n21\n00:01:18,415 --> 00:01:19,382\nSome caution.\n\n22\n00:01:19,812 --> 00:01:21,911\nThe authors call this research young.\n\n23\n00:01:22,384 --> 00:01:23,918\nMany studies were small,\n\n24\n00:01:24,049 --> 00:01:26,503\nanxiety was measured with surveys,\n\n25\n00:01:26,728 --> 00:01:30,828\nand a minority of very anxious students might respond differently.\n\n26\n00:01:30,998 --> 00:01:32,628\nThere is also a trade off.\n\n27\n00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:34,750\nHarder quizzes help learning more,\n\n28\n00:01:34,936 --> 00:01:38,483\nwhile easier, lower stakes quizzes calm anxiety more.\n\n29\n00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:42,477\nThe goal is a sweet spot: enough challenge to learn,\n\n30\n00:01:42,559 --> 00:01:44,511\nlow enough stakes to feel safe.\n\n31\n00:01:44,681 --> 00:01:45,444\nFor teaching.\n\n32\n00:01:45,785 --> 00:01:47,285\nStart the term with easy,\n\n33\n00:01:47,305 --> 00:01:50,467\nungraded quizzes, and raise the challenge gradually.\n\n34\n00:01:50,819 --> 00:01:53,688\nAllow retakes or drop the lowest scores.\n\n35\n00:01:54,034 --> 00:01:55,536\nTell students the purpose: practice,\n\n36\n00:01:55,761 --> 00:01:57,408\nnot judgment.\n\n37\n00:01:57,808 --> 00:02:03,208\nOne tip to share with students: frequent small quizzes make big exams less scary.\n\n38\n00:02:03,378 --> 00:02:05,204\nPractice makes tests familiar.\n\n39\n00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:07,191\nFamiliar is less frightening.\n\n"